  "conclusion": "Wall-clock: P 631.28s, B3 2.6s, B2 0.4s, B1 not measured. P is slower than B2: False, which is the expected observation at this size and the argument for local-mode routing (LR-07). P required 0 approval(s) and 0 other manual steps, so the first half of the hypothesis holds. Whole-pipeline test PR-AUC: P None (full feature catalogue), B3 None and B2 None (twelve features each) - not comparable as model quality, because the feature sets differ. Hand-written pipeline lines: B2 188, B3 201. P made 0 LLM calls, ran 4 sandboxed Spark jobs and wrote 15 audit entries and 6 decision records - the governance and evidence the baselines produce none of, which is the cost the wall-clock difference is buying. See the like-for-like trial for the only PR-AUC comparison in which the features are held constant.",
  "limitations": [
    "B1 is not measured: it is defined as a person following a runbook, and its metrics are engineer hours. Inventing them would fabricate the comparison.",
    "The dataset is TRIAD's synthetic generator, not Online Retail II, which has no customer, session, ticket or return tables and so cannot support the churn definition or most of the feature catalogue (ADR-0002).",
    "P made 0 LLM calls because no provider is configured (ADR-0003). The hypothesis that 'agent overhead dominates P's time' therefore cannot be tested for LLM time. The overhead that is present - validation gates including an sbt compile, sandbox container start-up per Spark job, governance and audit writes - is measured and reported instead, and is not a substitute for the untested claim."
  ],
